"""Original editorial series. Examples are invented; source facts are separately cited."""
DATE = '19 November 2026'
SOURCES = {
 'google-ai': ('Google: optimising for generative AI search', 'https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide'),
 'google-report': ('Google: Generative AI performance report', 'https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/16984139?hl=en'),
 'google-metrics': ('Google: Search performance report', 'https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7576553'),
 'nist': ('NIST: AI Risk Management Framework', 'https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework'),
 'study': ('Recallant: dated helpdesk study and method', 'https://recallant.com/helpdesk-study.html'),
 'ollama': ('Ollama: structured outputs', 'https://docs.ollama.com/capabilities/structured-outputs'),
 'content': ('Google: helpful, reliable, people-first content', 'https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content'),
}
def p(title, deck, text, takeaway, visual=None, refs=()):
 return dict(title=title, deck=deck, paragraphs=text.split('\n\n'), takeaway=takeaway, visual=visual, refs=list(refs))

ISSUES = [
 dict(slug='the-evidence-desk', title='The Evidence Desk', subtitle='Check an AI answer before it reaches a customer.', accent='#9b462d', motif='evidence', gist='A plausible answer needs a checkable source. This issue shows how to break a claim apart, inspect its support and decide what is safe to use.', pages=[
 p('The five-minute version', 'Start with the sentence that could cause trouble.', 'A small business does not need to investigate every adjective in a first draft. It does need to check statements that could change a customer’s decision: what is included, when something arrives, what it costs and whether it works with something they already own.\n\nOur running example is an invented two-person furniture workshop. An assistant drafts a product reply promising delivery, assembly and a warranty. The owner knows the product well, which makes the fluent reply especially tempting to send. Familiarity is useful; it is also how a new promise slips past.\n\nRead this issue with one draft open. Highlight its promises. For each, find the business record that supports it. If the record is missing, the answer is not ready. An AI reviewer can help find suspect sentences, but someone responsible for the business must settle the facts.', 'Keep supported facts; rewrite overstatements; hold unknowns; remove or correct contradictions.', 'triage'),
 p('One sentence. Three promises.', 'Grammar can hide several independent claims.', 'Invented draft: “Your desk arrives in three days, includes assembly and comes with a lifetime warranty.” That sentence has three claims. Each promise needs its own supporting record. A materials warranty, for example, may have a specific duration and exclusions.\n\nSeparate the clauses before checking. Copy each claim exactly into a small record, then add the source, its date and the relevant passage. Keep the original wording beside the proposed correction so a reviewer can see whether the meaning changed.\n\nDo not turn an unanswered question into a negative fact. “We could not verify assembly” differs from “assembly is not available”. The first describes our evidence; the second describes the service. Only the business can confirm the second. Keep the assembly specification in one maintained record so colleagues do not reuse conflicting versions.', 'Check the smallest claim that can be true or false.', 'claims'),
 p('Open the source', 'A link is a place to start reading.', 'For the workshop, the strongest source for the assembly promise is its current service specification. A blog about buying furniture cannot establish what this workshop supplies. A manufacturer’s page may establish a component specification. The seller’s current specification and service terms describe its advertised offer; applicable statutory rights still apply.\n\nRead beyond the search snippet. Check the page title, responsible organisation, publication or update date, applicable product and conditions. Find the passage that supports the exact claim, including exclusions. A source can be genuine and still be the wrong source for this question.\n\nIf a source cannot be opened, mark it unavailable. Keep the claim on hold when the consequence matters; do not allow the assistant to replace the missing evidence with a more confident paraphrase. A second AI repeating the answer is not an independent source.', 'Ask: does this source establish this claim for this product, place and date?', 'source'),
 p('The claim ledger', 'A tiny table is more useful than a vague confidence score.', 'Illustrative workshop record: the workshop’s current service specification says “dispatch within three working days”; the draft says “arrives in three days”. Dispatch and arrival are different events. The corrected answer should use the actual dispatch commitment and explain how delivery is confirmed.\n\nThe assembly line has no supporting record. Mark it “unverified” and ask the owner. The warranty document specifies a duration and exclusions, so replace “lifetime” with the verified terms or link the current warranty without summarising beyond what has been checked.\n\nA good ledger records the decision as well as the problem. Otherwise the next editor repeats the investigation. Use four evidence states: supported, narrower than the draft, unavailable and contradicted. Map them to publication actions: keep, rewrite to the supported scope, hold, and remove or correct. If the source itself is wrong, its owner must correct it first.', 'Keep the original claim, evidence, correction and responsible reviewer together.', 'ledger'),
 p('The date is part of the fact', 'A correct answer can expire.', 'The workshop changes its assembly service for a new delivery area. Yesterday’s approved reply may now be wrong for some orders. An approval should therefore name the version of the source, the date checked and any condition that limits reuse.\n\nSet review triggers around changes that matter: a new supplier, changed service area, price revision or revised warranty. A monthly reminder can help, but it does not replace a trigger when the underlying promise changes tomorrow.\n\nFor external rules, find the responsible authority and applicable jurisdiction. High-stakes legal, medical or financial questions require qualified review; this issue is an editorial workflow, not advice on those subjects. NIST’s voluntary risk framework is a useful reference for thinking about consequences and oversight. It is not a certification earned by using this worksheet.', 'Give reusable copy an owner and an expiry trigger.', 'timeline', ('nist',)),
 p('Rewrite without adding promises', 'Give the reader a specific fact and a clear next step.', 'Invented original: “The perfect desk for every home office. Delivered in three days with free assembly.” A cautious rewrite is not simply “The excellent desk for your home office”. That leaves the practical questions unanswered.\n\nA better illustrative reply: “This model is 120 cm wide. Our current specification lists dispatch within three working days. Ask us to confirm delivery timing and assembly for your address before ordering.” The measurements and dispatch statement would still need checking against the real product record.\n\nNotice what the revision does: it gives one useful specification, preserves the difference between dispatch and delivery, and names the open question. It does not invent a guarantee to make the paragraph sound decisive. The reader now knows what is established and what to ask.', 'A useful answer can include an honest unresolved question.', 'beforeafter'),
 p('Use a second reader well', 'Ask for disagreement, then resolve it.', 'Give a reviewer the draft and the supporting records. Ask them to identify unsupported promises, altered numbers, missing conditions and statements whose source says something narrower. Request the exact sentence and the reason, not an overall score.\n\nWhen two reviewers disagree, return to the source. A majority vote between models cannot change a warranty. If the document is ambiguous, the business owner decides what can actually be offered and updates the source.\n\nUse non-sensitive examples when trying a new review tool. Check the service’s current data terms before uploading customer information. Inspect the reviewer’s output before accepting its recommendation. Record the disagreement and fix so the same weak sentence does not survive in the next brochure.', 'A reviewer must be able to point to the sentence and the evidence.', 'review'),
 p('A reusable check sheet', 'Complete this before a consequential claim leaves the desk.', 'Record the asset and intended audience. List each material claim separately. For each claim, record the source URL or internal document version, the exact supporting passage, product or geographic scope, date checked and any exclusions.\n\nThen record the decision: keep, rewrite, hold, or remove/correct. Save the final wording and the person responsible for approving the underlying business fact. If the source is unavailable, say so explicitly; an empty cell is too easy to mistake for a completed check.\n\nFinally, compare the finished graphic or PDF with the approved text. Layout changes can drop a footnote, truncate a condition or leave an old price in a corner. The text file may be correct while the published image makes a different promise.', 'Review the final exported artifact, not only the manuscript.', 'worksheet'),
 p('Know when to stop', 'The cost of checking should match the consequence.', 'A harmless brainstorming list can carry unresolved ideas. A public product promise cannot. Separate exploratory drafts from material ready for customers, and make that status visible in the filename or work board.\n\nIf the evidence is unavailable and the claim is optional, remove it. If the claim is essential to the customer’s decision, ask the responsible person and hold the answer. Repeatedly asking different models until one supplies a pleasant answer is not verification; it is shopping for reassurance.\n\nThis method does not establish that every source is honest or complete. It reduces avoidable gaps between a statement and the records used to support it. For decisions with serious consequences, qualified domain review remains necessary even when the citations look tidy.', 'When the missing fact matters, “not ready” is a useful result.', 'stop'),
 p('Put it to work this week', 'Choose one frequently reused answer.', 'Take a product reply, proposal paragraph or website FAQ that customers actually read. Check its material promises using the worksheet. Correct the source record first if the business itself has changed; then update the copy and every active version that reuses it.\n\nKeep one short change note: what was wrong, what supports the correction and where the corrected text now appears. This is enough to make the next review easier without creating a paperwork business inside your business.\n\nFor the invented workshop, the useful outcome is modest: customers receive an accurate dispatch statement and a clear way to confirm assembly. We have not measured conversion, prevented a real complaint or tested this example on an AI system. The example teaches a checking method; it is not a customer result.', 'Finish with a corrected answer and a source record someone else can follow.', 'action'),
 ]),
 dict(slug='the-tuesday-test', title='The Tuesday Test', subtitle='Choose an AI tool using the work you actually do.', accent='#236a75', motif='tools', gist='Run a small, repeatable trial. Count corrections, handling time and total cost before choosing a tool.', pages=[
 p('Start with an ordinary Tuesday', 'Two-minute summary: define the job, test it, count the repairs.', 'A furniture workshop needs to turn rough order notes into a production brief. A brilliant essay or dramatic generated video tells the owner little about whether a tool can preserve dimensions, flag a missing finish and avoid inventing a delivery date.\n\nWrite the job in a sentence: “Create a draft production brief from these approved order notes; flag anything missing and do not place an order.” Then define a usable result. The brief must preserve every supplied measurement, separate confirmed choices from questions and be easy for a colleague to check.\n\nUse this issue to design a small trial before making a commitment. The proposed trial is an editorial method, not a validated industry benchmark. It can reveal obvious mismatches; it cannot prove reliability on work you did not test.', 'Judge the output you need, with the constraints you actually have.', 'task'),
 p('Write the acceptance rule first', 'Decide what failure means before seeing the output.', 'For the invented production brief, a changed dimension is a hard failure. Attractive formatting cannot compensate. Missing an explicitly supplied finish is also a failure. A correct brief with clumsy headings may be repairable.\n\nMake these distinctions before testing. Otherwise a charming answer quietly changes the criteria. Write a few non-negotiables, then a small set of preferences such as readable structure, correction time and export convenience.\n\nA tool that does not meet a privacy or permission requirement should not proceed to a trial with sensitive material. Start with synthetic order notes. Check current vendor terms for the specific plan and feature; consumer chat, API and business plans can differ. Recheck prices when making the decision; they change.', 'Use hard failures for material errors; preferences for convenience.', 'gates'),
 p('Build a small test pack', 'Include the awkward cases.', 'Prepare a normal order, a missing measurement, a contradictory delivery note and an empty input. Keep one correct reference brief for each case. The empty case should produce a request for information, not an invented order.\n\nFreeze the input text and instructions so competing tools receive the same task. Record the product, version where visible, settings, date and whether web access or extra tools were enabled. If the interface does not expose a model version, record that limitation.\n\nKeep a few cases aside until after you adjust the instructions. These held-out cases help reveal whether the prompt has become a clever fit to the examples already seen. A handful of cases screens out obvious mismatches. Establishing broader reliability takes substantially more evidence.', 'Normal, missing, contradictory and empty inputs all belong in the trial.', 'testpack'),
 p('A tidy structure can still be wrong', 'Schema checks and fact checks answer different questions.', 'A production brief can contain every required field and still change 120 cm to 120 mm. A format checker can establish that a field exists or contains a number. It cannot, by itself, establish that the number came from the order.\n\nSome tools can constrain output to a fixed set of fields, often called a schema. Ollama’s documentation is one technical example, cited for the mechanism rather than as a product recommendation. A completed brief still needs checking against its input.\n\nCheck meaning separately: every source measurement preserved, no invented deadline, unresolved details labelled, no action taken without authority. Treat a well-formed file as one stage of acceptance. The colleague using it should not have to discover the difference with a saw.', 'Valid structure is necessary for some workflows; it is not factual accuracy.', 'structure', ('ollama',)),
 p('Count the repair work', 'Fast generation can produce slow work.', 'Illustrative timing: a manual brief takes 12 minutes. An assisted draft takes 2 minutes to prepare, 1 minute to generate and 6 minutes to inspect and correct. Total: 9 minutes. The useful recurring saving is 3 minutes. Record one-off setup and test-pack preparation separately before estimating payback.\n\nRepeat the measurement across ordinary and awkward cases. Include retries, transfers between tools and the time spent investigating a plausible error. Report failures rather than dropping them from the average.\n\nKeep quality alongside time. A faster process that silently changes a dimension is unacceptable, however attractive its average. Do not multiply a tiny pilot’s time saving across a year as though every task, colleague and deadline behaves the same way. Use the trial to decide what deserves a longer test.', 'Total handling time = preparation + generation + checking + correction.', 'timebars'),
 p('Compare outputs without the badge', 'Preference can arrive before evidence.', 'Where practical, remove tool names from the outputs and ask a second person to assess them against the acceptance rules. Keep the original inputs beside each output. The evaluator must be able to verify the content, not merely choose the nicest prose.\n\nRecord disagreements by case. One reviewer may prefer a shorter brief while another spots an omitted instruction. Resolve hard failures first; discuss style after the facts are secure.\n\nIf only one person is available, score the outputs before revealing their source where possible and revisit borderline cases after a break. This is not full blinding or a controlled scientific study. It is a modest way to make personal enthusiasm less influential. Keep a short explanation for each score so another person can understand it later.', 'Score against the task, and retain the reasons for disagreement.', 'review'),
 p('Build a shortlist you can finish', 'Start with tools you already have.', 'Check the tools you already pay for before looking for another subscription. Can one produce the required output, export it into your existing process and work within your data rules? Remove candidates that fail a mandatory requirement before uploading real material.\n\nKeep the first shortlist to two or three candidates. Add a new tool only when it fills a specific gap, such as an essential file format or an approved offline workflow. A colleague’s recommendation can suggest a candidate; your own test decides whether it fits.\n\nFor local versus hosted options, inspect the full data path and operating effort. Local work still uses hardware, electricity and maintenance; plugins can send data elsewhere. Hosted work brings service limits and external terms. Write down the constraint you need to test, then run the same task under that constraint.', 'Shortlist by requirements, then test the actual task.', 'tradeoff'),
 p('A two-week trial plan', 'Keep the experiment small enough to finish.', 'Suggested calendar: days 1–2, define the rules and test pack; days 3–6, run the same pack and review every output; days 7–10, investigate failures and repeat with held-out cases. Use days 11–14 for supervised suitable work and the decision.\n\nUse the remaining time for a supervised trial on suitable real work, only after privacy and permissions are checked. Keep the existing method available. Record what happened when the tool failed, not just what happened when it worked.\n\nAt the end, choose adopt narrowly, extend the test, or reject. Do not let a trial become a subscription by inertia. Check renewal and cancellation terms before starting any paid trial. The calendar is a suggested organising aid, not a claim that two weeks is enough for every business or risk level.', 'Make the final decision date and the fallback method explicit.', 'calendar'),
 p('The decision sheet', 'Keep evidence beside the recommendation.', 'For each tool, record the exact task, configuration, date, tested cases, hard failures, median handling time if useful, observed cost and unresolved risks. Separate measured amounts from estimates and include the basis for any conversion to monthly cost.\n\nIf the process handles customer information, add the approved data scope and retention requirements. If it can send messages or change records, state who authorises those actions and how duplicates are prevented. A good text generator is not automatically a safe publisher.\n\nConclude with one bounded recommendation: for example, “Use for draft production briefs with human measurement checks; do not send directly to production.” That tells the next operator more than a five-star rating. Repeat the relevant checks when the task, model or integration changes materially.', 'Approve a specific use, with limits, rather than declaring a universal winner.', 'worksheet'),
 p('A useful failure is still useful', 'Reject a tool when the evidence calls for it.', 'Suppose a tool handles normal notes well but invents a finish whenever the order is incomplete. The answer is not to hide the awkward cases. Either improve the workflow and retest on held-out inputs, or keep that tool away from this job.\n\nYou may discover that a form with required fields solves the problem better than an AI assistant. That is a good outcome. The aim was a reliable production brief, not a more impressive software collection.\n\nThis issue draws on task-based evaluation principles used in our internal tool-selection work. It does not publish private benchmark results or claim that any named product won a fresh comparison. Your decision needs your own task evidence, including the inconvenient failures.', 'The cheapest useful result may be deciding not to automate this task.', 'action'),
 ]),
 dict(slug='named-cited-chosen', title='Named. Cited. Chosen?', subtitle='Read AI visibility without mistaking attention for a recommendation.', accent='#806000', motif='visibility', gist='Separate names, citations, recommendations and enquiries. Record where and when an answer appeared, then improve the facts a buyer needs.', pages=[
 p('The short version', 'A mention tells you less than it appears to.', 'An AI answer names a furniture workshop. Is that good news? Perhaps. It might be a recommendation, a comparison, a warning or a passing reference. The useful evidence is the sentence around the name and the question that produced it.\n\nThis issue gives a small business a proportionate way to inspect that evidence. Use it after checking the accuracy of your own website and listings. Start with the questions customers actually ask and the business facts you can maintain.\n\nA periodic check may be enough when there is little evidence that AI discovery matters to your customers. Increase attention only when the information could change a business decision. Treat a dramatic screenshot as one observation and ask whether it changes a practical decision.', 'Read the answer in context before turning it into a metric.', 'ladder'),
 p('Five different observations', 'Keep each label attached to its evidence.', 'A mention means the brand is named. A citation means a source is linked or otherwise identified. A recommendation requires wording that expresses a preference or suitable choice. First-named order records position in the text; it does not establish endorsement. An enquiry is a separate event in the business’s own records.\n\nThese observations can coexist, but none guarantees the next. An answer can cite your website while recommending another supplier. It can recommend your service without linking your domain. A prospective buyer can read an answer and contact you later through an untracked route.\n\nKeep a copy of the complete answer and its sources. If a dashboard supplies only a score, ask how that score is calculated and inspect examples. A number without a definition is mostly typography.', 'Mention, citation, recommendation, order and enquiry are separate fields.', 'ladder'),
 p('An answer under the magnifier', 'Invented example, not a captured AI result.', 'Question: “What should I check before ordering a desk for a small room?” Invented answer: “Measure the usable space and access route. Workshop A offers a compact model. Compare its dimensions and assembly terms with other suppliers before ordering.”\n\nWorkshop A is named. The wording does not establish that it is the best option. If a link points to Workshop A’s specification page, that would be an owned-site citation, but the answer still asks the reader to compare.\n\nNow change the sentence to “For the stated width limit, Workshop A is the best fit among these options.” That expresses a preference within stated conditions. In a real AI answer, that wording would express a recommendation to verify, not proof of product quality or a customer purchase. The words around the brand do the work.', 'Classify what the answer actually says, including its conditions.', 'answer'),
 p('Choose questions before collecting answers', 'Avoid building a test around the result you want.', 'Write a small panel of genuine buyer questions: suitability, alternatives, comparisons and practical requirements. Separate questions that name the brand from those that do not. A prompt containing your own name naturally changes what an answer might discuss.\n\nRecord the language, market, surface and settings. Consumer chat and API outputs should not be treated as interchangeable observations. Note whether browsing was enabled and whether the session carried earlier context.\n\nRepeat questions consistently if repeatability matters, while retaining every attempt and failure. Do not keep asking until a pleasing answer appears and then call that the baseline. A practical panel samples specific questions. Its coverage of customer demand and possible answers remains limited.', 'Freeze the question panel and collection conditions before comparison.', 'sampling'),
 p('The denominator deserves a label', 'A failed capture is not a negative answer.', 'Invented collection: 10 attempted captures; 2 fail; 8 return usable answers; 3 of those mention the workshop. The observed mention fraction is 3 of 8 usable answers, or 37.5%. Report the 2 failures alongside it. Do not quietly call the result 3 of 10 unless you explicitly define a different attempted-capture metric.\n\nSmall samples are volatile. One extra mention would move this example to 4 of 8. That is a large percentage change with very little underlying evidence. Repeated answers from one setup may also be dependent, so simple statistical assumptions need care.\n\nUse counts and descriptions before ranking brands by small differences. The purpose is to find inspectable patterns and questions worth investigating, not to manufacture a precise-looking league table.', 'Show attempted, failed, usable and positive counts separately.', 'denominator'),
 p('Reading the October study’s scope', 'A worked example of checking the population behind a chart.', 'Recallant’s published helpdesk study records 44 questions repeated five times, producing 220 answers collected on 6 October 2026. The public explorer uses a 40-question non-branded subset. Those populations must not be mixed.\n\nAutomated first-named order in that record is a positional measure, not a verified recommendation. The study also reports a separate ten-answer manual ChatGPT check; its different collection method makes it a separate observation. For a concrete scope check, 44 minus 40 leaves four questions outside that explorer subset. A chart of the subset cannot answer a question about all 44 without examining the exclusions.\n\nThis magazine does not rerun that research or claim the results describe today’s answers. It uses the study as an example of why dates, question sets and definitions belong beside a chart. It is not evidence of a client outcome or of an improvement caused by Recallant.', 'Historical observations stay dated, scoped and separate from current claims.', 'study', ('study',)),
 p('Google reporting, checked November 2026', 'A dedicated view with a defined measurement scope.', 'Google now documents a dedicated Generative AI performance report for Search, covering impressions in AI Overviews and AI Mode. Its help page states worldwide rollout as of 31 August 2026. A missing report can reflect insufficient impressions.\n\nThe report’s documented metric is impressions, not a separate AI click or revenue measure. Its data is included in the broader Web performance reporting: do not add it to the overall total as extra traffic. Check date, country, device and page scope when comparing views.\n\nThis section was checked on 19 November 2026 against the current report reference, including its “Not seeing the report?” and “More about the data” sections. Recheck that reference before building a recurring report around a tutorial or screenshot.', 'A new reporting view does not create a new revenue attribution method.', 'report', ('google-report',)),
 p('Improve the page a buyer lands on', 'Answer the practical questions you can control.', 'For our invented workshop, the useful page explains dimensions, materials, delivery coverage, assembly and how to confirm a custom order. It shows the actual item and links the applicable terms. These details help a buyer assess fit even when no AI system chooses to cite the page.\n\nGoogle’s current guidance continues to connect AI-search visibility with SEO fundamentals, useful original content and relevant images or video. It cautions against mass-producing query variations primarily to manipulate results.\n\nA small business can therefore start with one important page and its genuine information gaps. Collect the facts from the people doing the work. Add photographs that show the feature being discussed, rather than a decorative image that merely shares the mood. No page format guarantees inclusion.', 'Make the page useful on its own; discovery is not guaranteed.', 'page', ('google-ai',)),
 p('The visibility record', 'A practical worksheet for a bounded check.', 'Record the buyer question, reason for including it, exact wording, language, market, product surface, collection date and relevant settings. Save the complete answer and cited URLs where permitted. Mark failed or unavailable captures explicitly.\n\nClassify mention, owned-domain citation, recommendation wording and first-named position separately. Add the sentence supporting each classification and note ambiguous cases. Let another reader challenge the labels before aggregating them.\n\nFinish with the business implication. Does the answer misstate an important specification? Is the correct source absent from your own page? Or is the observation interesting but not actionable? Keep this decision alongside the evidence so another round of collection has a purpose.', 'Every metric should lead back to an answer and a defined question.', 'worksheet'),
 p('A sensible next move', 'Fix a fact before buying a bigger number.', 'Choose one recurring information gap and correct the source you control. For the workshop, that might be a delivery page that never says which areas it serves. Record the change date and check that the page works for a person on a phone.\n\nIf you later repeat an answer check, retain the original panel and note what else changed. Different answers after a page edit do not prove the edit caused them. Product updates, source changes and ordinary variation remain plausible explanations.\n\nFor the next question—whether visibility leads to useful conversations—turn to The Enquiry Ledger. It uses the business’s own records rather than repeating the answer sample. Keep those two investigations distinct.', 'Choose one useful change, then observe without inventing causality.', 'action'),
 ]),
 dict(slug='the-enquiry-ledger', title='The Enquiry Ledger', subtitle='Find out which attention becomes a useful conversation.', accent='#3a6657', motif='enquiry', gist='Define an enquiry, keep unknown sources visible and follow the conversation through to its actual outcome.', pages=[
 p('Begin where the customer arrives', 'Two-minute summary: follow the enquiry through to its outcome.', 'A workshop receives a form submission, a phone call and a social message. Those are three contacts, but they may concern one project. Counting them as three leads makes the chart look busy and the business harder to understand.\n\nDefine the unit you want to track: a distinct enquiry about a possible purchase. Keep the contact history together, with a simple internal reference. Then decide what makes an enquiry relevant to the service you can actually deliver.\n\nThis issue uses invented workshop records to explain a modest enquiry log. It does not claim to reconstruct every customer journey or assign revenue to AI search. The useful outcome is a clearer picture of what arrived, what happened next and where the business is losing track.', 'Count distinct enquiries consistently, not every message as a new lead.', 'journey'),
 p('Write the qualification rule', 'A useful conversation needs a shared definition.', 'For the invented workshop, a qualified enquiry includes a product need the workshop serves, a location it can deliver to and enough detail to discuss the next step. A spam message, duplicate or unrelated supplier pitch does not qualify.\n\nThese are example criteria, not universal rules. A new business may need to learn from incomplete enquiries rather than dismiss them. Use statuses such as needs clarification, relevant, outside scope and closed, with a short reason.\n\nDo not make the website form collect every possible detail merely to improve a spreadsheet. Ask what is necessary to respond, and gather further context in the conversation. Keep personal information limited to a legitimate business need, with access and retention appropriate to your obligations.', 'Write a definition your future self can apply the same way.', 'qualification'),
 p('Let unknown stay unknown', 'Missing attribution is information about your measurement.', 'Illustrative month: 20 distinct enquiries. Six people report a referral, five report search, two report social media, one reports an AI chat tool and six give no usable source. These categories are self-reported first discovery in this example, not verified click paths.\n\nThe unknown group is 6 of 20, or 30%. Keep it visible. Distributing those six across the other categories would create evidence that was never collected. A customer who says “search” may have encountered an AI Overview; this self-report alone cannot separate that exposure.\n\nA customer may encounter several channels. Keep “how they first heard of us” separate from “what brought them here today” if both matter. Do not force a complicated journey into one confident label just because a chart needs colours.', 'Unknown is a valid category, not a spreadsheet error to hide.', 'channels'),
 p('Three records, three different views', 'Search data, website data and enquiry data are not interchangeable.', 'Search Console can describe search impressions and clicks within its reporting definitions. Website analytics can describe observed activity subject to configuration, consent and other gaps. The enquiry log records the conversations the business actually receives.\n\nA discrepancy between these systems is not automatically a bug. They use different units, scopes, time zones and collection methods. A search click and a recorded session each describe a different event from a qualified enquiry. A blank referrer cannot establish direct discovery or AI discovery.\n\nGoogle defines CTR as clicks divided by impressions in its Search performance report. Use that ratio for its intended scope. Do not divide an unrelated enquiry count by a different population of impressions and label the result a trustworthy conversion rate. First establish whether the records can be compared.', 'Name the unit, period, scope and missing data beside every figure.', 'records', ('google-metrics',)),
 p('Lower rate. More qualified enquiries.', 'Compare relevant volume alongside the percentage.', 'Illustrative enquiry figures: source A brings five enquiries, four qualified under the agreed rule: 80%. Source B brings 30 enquiries, 12 qualified: 40%. Source B has the lower qualification rate and three times as many qualified enquiries. Neither figure tells us the cost or eventual value of the work.\n\nBefore reacting, compare equivalent date ranges, relevant pages and the same metric definitions. Look for changes in the mix of services requested and the qualification criteria. A broad average can move because the audience changed, even if the offer stayed the same.\n\nFor a small business, a plain table of counts often beats a dramatic percentage arrow. Include the denominator and resist rounding away the small sample. A single additional enquiry could move the smaller sample’s rate sharply.', 'Rate and volume answer different questions. Keep both.', 'qualified'),
 p('Inspect the handover', 'A working button is only the beginning.', 'Test the journey a genuine prospect takes: read the offer, find the next step, submit the enquiry, receive confirmation and get a useful response. Use a clearly labelled test and avoid sending unnecessary personal information.\n\nDistinguish what you observed. A successful browser message is not proof of inbox delivery. Inbox receipt is not proof that someone replied. A reply is not a booked call, and a booked call is not a sale. Record each event separately.\n\nCheck the fallback route too. A visible business email and clear expectation about the next step can help when a form fails. Do not promise response times the business cannot support. The measurement system should expose a missed handover rather than compensate with a nicer traffic chart.', 'Verify delivery and follow-up, not just the submit animation.', 'journey'),
 p('Change one useful thing', 'Give the experiment a question.', 'Suppose enquiries repeatedly ask whether assembly is included. A bounded change is to add a clear assembly explanation beside the desk specification and link the full terms. The hypothesis is that customers will arrive with fewer unresolved assembly questions.\n\nRecord the change, date, intended audience and what you will observe. Keep the enquiry question field consistent. Note other changes such as a promotion, service-area expansion or new campaign that could affect the comparison.\n\nA before-and-after log can guide decisions, but it does not establish causation. With low enquiry volumes, qualitative notes may be more useful than a percentage. Read the actual questions and ask whether the new explanation resolved them.', 'State the question, the change and the observation before looking at results.', 'experiment'),
 p('Read a small result honestly', 'Read the actual cases before drawing a conclusion.', 'Invented example: before the page edit, 3 of 8 enquiries ask about assembly. After it, 1 of 6 does. It is tempting to call the change a large improvement. The counts are small, the people differ and other conditions may have changed.\n\nA defensible note says: “Fewer assembly questions appeared in this small later sample. We will continue observing and inspect whether the page answers the question clearly.” It does not claim a proven conversion uplift or annual revenue gain.\n\nKeep the page if it makes the service clearer and accurately reflects the offer. Evidence can support a sensible content decision without supporting a strong causal claim. You do not need a fake experiment result to justify answering a real customer question.', 'Describe what happened, then separate the possible explanation.', 'smallnumbers'),
 p('The weekly ledger', 'A short review that ends with an action.', 'For each distinct enquiry, keep the date, internal reference, requested service, source as reported or observed, qualification status, next action, responsible person and outcome. Avoid putting unnecessary personal details in a shared reporting sheet.\n\nOnce a week, reconcile duplicates and read the unresolved cases. Which enquiries are waiting for a reply? Which question recurs? Which offer attracts people outside your scope? Which source is still unknown?\n\nUse a short summary: counts by status, repeated questions, overdue actions and one proposed improvement. Keep booked work and revenue separate until they are confirmed in the business records. Track proposals sent and invoices paid as separate milestones.', 'The next action and its owner matter as much as the source.', 'worksheet'),
 p('A month with fewer guesses', 'Keep the loop small enough to maintain.', 'Week one: define the enquiry and check delivery. Week two: review recurring questions and choose one accurate page improvement. Week three: inspect the new conversations and any operational problems. Week four: summarise what you know, what remains unknown and what to try next.\n\nThis is a suggested rhythm, not a promised growth timetable. Some businesses receive too few enquiries for a useful monthly comparison; extend the period and retain the individual notes.\n\nThe workshop’s goal is a clearer buying conversation and fewer dropped handovers. Search visibility is one possible contributor. The business still needs a suitable offer, delivery capacity and follow-through.', 'Close with a decision and its owner.', 'action'),
 ]),
]

